  data:  # 9dd1
    kind: work_item  # 9dd1
    title: "taint does not propagate through first-party THIN WRAPPERS around catalogued primitives (kserve run() -> subprocess.run) - 3 real flows lost to WI-damir's gate"  # 9dd1
    status: todo_hard  # 9dd1
    priority: 2  # 9dd1
    parent:  # 9dd1
    tags:  # 9dd1
    - analysis_quality  # 9dd1
    - adr_0017  # 9dd1
    isbefore: []  # 9dd1
    duplicate_of: []  # 9dd1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 9dd1
    pr_ref:  # 9dd1
    description: "FOUND BY THE WI-damir VALIDATION COHORT 2026-08-02, as the one class of flow\nthat fix legitimately loses. Filed rather than folded in, because the fix\nbelongs in propagation, not in the matching gate.\n\nWHAT HAPPENS. kserve/hack/setup/scripts/generate-versions-from-gomod.py:52:\n\n    def run(cmd):\n        return subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True, capture_output=True,\n                              text=True, check=True).stdout\n\nA first-party ONE-LINE WRAPPER around the catalogued primitive. Before WI-damir\nthe engine matched the wrapper `run` to the catalog's `subprocess.run` BY NAME\nand reported flows through it. That was accidentally right in effect -- data\nreally does reach subprocess -- and wrong in mechanism: the wrapper is not the\nprimitive, and the same name-only match is what reported caddy's\n`func Log() *zap.Logger` as a logging sink and d3's logarithm as console.log.\n\nWI-damir correctly refuses to call the wrapper the primitive. The flow is then\nlost because nothing propagates THROUGH the wrapper body to the real\nsubprocess.run call inside it. Measured cost on the 9-repo cohort: 3 flows of\nthe 23 removed (kserve subprocess.run x2, json.dump x1). The other 20 removals\nwere verified false against the source.\n\nTHIS IS THE SAME SHAPE AS WI-nibav (defusedcsv), and the pair is the point: both\nare cases where substring/name-only matching produced a right-ish answer by a\nwrong mechanism, and tightening the mechanism exposes a real gap that has to be\nclosed somewhere else. WI-nibav's home is the catalog; this one's is\ninterprocedural propagation.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not implemented). The call graph already contains\nget_helm_versions -> run -> subprocess.run, so the information is present; what\nis missing is that a first-party function which reaches a catalogued sink should\nmake its own CALLERS reach it too. Options to weigh, not a decision:\n  (a) transitive closure -- treat a first-party function that calls a sink as a\n      sink for its callers. Simple; risks a very wide blast radius.\n  (b) propagate only through THIN wrappers (single-statement bodies that return\n      or forward the primitive's result). Narrow, matches the observed shape,\n      and is checkable from the AST.\n  (c) leave it, and disclose that flows through first-party wrappers are not\n      reported.\nPrefer (b) on current evidence, but MEASURE the blast radius of (a) on the\ncohort before choosing -- the cohort A/B harness for this exists\n(scratchpad cohort-after.sh / ab2.py) and gives a per-flow diff.\n\nDO NOT close this by relaxing WI-damir's gate. That gate removed 20 verified\nfalse positives including INV-karud's headline example; restoring 3 real flows\nby readmitting 20 false ones is a bad trade and reproduces the defect."  # 9dd1
    fields: {}  # 9dd1
- op: update  # e3d4
  at: '2026-08-05T01:31:23Z'  # e3d4
  by: agent  # e3d4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e3d4
  clock: 2  # e3d4
  nonce: e3d4  # e3d4
  set:  # e3d4
    priority: 3  # e3d4
    description: "RE-SCOPED 2026-08-04. THE FILED EVIDENCE IS REFUTED. The thesis may still be\nreal, but nothing in this item demonstrated it and the repro cannot demonstrate\nit even in principle. Original filing retained at the bottom.\n\n=== WHAT THE FILING CLAIMED ===\nThat WI-damir legitimately lost 3 real flows on kserve (subprocess.run x2,\njson.dump x1) because a first-party ONE-LINE WRAPPER around a catalogued\nprimitive stops taint propagating THROUGH its body:\n\n    def run(cmd):\n        return subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True, capture_output=True,\n                              text=True, check=True).stdout\n\nand that this was \"accidentally right in effect -- data really does reach\nsubprocess\".\n\n=== WHAT THE ARTIFACTS ACTUALLY SHOW ===\nRe-checked against the item's own filed repro, on the real kserve map, by\ndiffing the preserved before/after cohort arms rather than re-reasoning.\n\nALL THREE lost rows carry `source_primitive: \"run\"`, and the source_symbol is\nthe wrapper's CALLER:\n\n  {source_primitive: run, source_symbol: ...:69-84:get_helm_versions:function,\n   sink_symbol: python:subprocess:0-0:run:external_symbol}\n  {source_primitive: run, source_symbol: ...:188-193:ensure_helm_repo:function,\n   sink_symbol: python:subprocess:0-0:run:external_symbol}\n  {source_primitive: run, source_symbol: ...:69-84:get_helm_versions:function,\n   sink_symbol: python:json:0-0:dump:external_symbol}\n\nThe python catalogue declares exactly two `run` SOURCES:\n\n    flask.Flask.run    untrusted_input\n    uvicorn.run        untrusted_input\n\nSo a local `def run(cmd)` in a helm-version generation script was matched BY\nNAME to a Flask/uvicorn web-server entrypoint and thereby declared a source of\nuntrusted input. That is a textbook WI-damir false positive -- the same shape as\nd3's logarithm reported as console.log and caddy's `func Log() *zap.Logger`\nreported as a logging sink. It is not a loss; it is a removal that was correct\nfor the reason WI-damir was built.\n\nTHE SINK SIDE WAS NEVER BROKEN, which is the half the filing got backwards.\nVerified live on the current tree:\n  * the edge `run -> python:subprocess:0-0:run:external_symbol` EXISTS\n    (type=calls, is_resolved=False) in the kserve map;\n  * `_match_propagation_entry` matches that external id to `subprocess.run`\n    (zone=subprocess) today;\n  * so the wrapper IS registered in `sink_callers`, and propagation never needed\n    to go THROUGH the wrapper body -- a source reaching `get_helm_versions`\n    would BFS forward to `run` and the flow would be emitted with the wrapper on\n    the path.\nThe filing's proposed fixes (a) transitive closure and (b) thin-wrapper\npropagation were both aimed at a sink-side problem that does not exist.\n\nWHY NO REAL FLOW EXISTS HERE AT ALL: `subprocess` appears ZERO times in\npython's 83 catalogued sources. It is sink-only (25 subprocess-zone sinks). So\nthere is no untrusted-input source anywhere in that file, and no correct\nanalysis can report a flow from it. The claim now reads `confirmed` with 0\nevidence rows, which is the right answer.\n\n=== WHAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT ITEM SHAPE ===\nThe SOURCE-side wrapper gap is real in principle and is NOT what was filed.\n`source_callers` is built by scanning edges whose dst matches a source, and the\nBFS then runs FORWARD from those callers. So when a first-party function calls a\ncatalogued source and RETURNS it, only that function becomes a source caller --\nits own callers, which receive the tainted return value, are upstream and are\nnever visited. `reverse_adj` is built at taint.py:1409 and READ BY NOBODY, which\nis the capability that would serve it.\n\nThat gap has NO measured instance. It cannot be demonstrated on this repro\nbecause subprocess is not a source. Before working it:\n  1. find a repro where a first-party function calls a CATALOGUED source and\n     returns it, and a caller of that function reaches a sink;\n  2. confirm the flow is missed;\n  3. only then choose a fix.\nFiling a fix shape here would repeat this item's own error.\n\nSTATUS lowered to P3 and the priority reasoning is explicit: the item retains a\nplausible thesis with zero measured instances, which ranks below WI-nibav and\nINV-mivud (both of which have concrete, verified instances).\n\n=== ADJACENT QUESTION THIS SURFACED, FILED SEPARATELY ===\nWhether subprocess OUTPUT should be a catalogued untrusted_input source is a\nreal threat-model question (command output is untrusted in most models) and\nwould make the kserve case a genuine flow. That is a catalogue decision with\nsecurity consequences, not a propagation bug.\n\n=== ORIGINAL FILING, RETAINED FOR THE RECORD ===\nFOUND BY THE WI-damir VALIDATION COHORT 2026-08-02, as the one class of flow\nthat fix legitimately loses. Filed rather than folded in, because the fix\nbelongs in propagation, not in the matching gate. [Body preserved in git\nhistory and in the discussion thread; the operative claims are quoted and\nanswered above.]"  # e3d4
